# Review standard (internal-review-repo)

Source: the `Research objective` issue (#1), the brief (idea-repo ef3c571,
requirements D1, S1-S3, R1-R2, C1-C4, P1-P5) and the work assignment
(story-repo bdd8805). Where they differ, the objective wins.

The assembled delivery is judged against each item below. Every item is
checked on the delivered artifacts themselves at the delivered revisions
(PDF opened page by page, figure inspected, code re-run locally, LaTeX
re-compiled), not on assertions in issues or commit messages.

## R1 Data
- Iris fetched from OpenML itself, dataset id 61 (code shows the OpenML
  call/URL; dataset version and a checksum or row/class counts recorded:
  150 rows, 4 features, 3 classes of 50).
- No other copy of Iris (e.g. `sklearn.datasets.load_iris`) used for any number.

## R2 Methods
- Multinomial logistic regression (a softmax model, not one-vs-rest); solver,
  regularisation and preprocessing stated.
- k-NN with k chosen by inner cross-validation on the outer training folds
  only (nested CV, no leakage); k grid and inner scheme stated; the k values
  actually chosen are reported or available.
- Any preprocessing (e.g. standardisation) is fit inside the pipeline on
  training data only.

## R3 Protocol
- 10 repetitions x stratified 5-fold = 50 outer test folds; both methods
  evaluated on the same splits (so the difference is paired); seeds fixed and
  stated.

## R4 Statistics
- Each method: mean accuracy with a 95% interval; paired difference
  (method A - method B, direction stated) with a 95% interval.
- The interval method is stated and suits the dependence among CV folds
  (overlapping training sets, repeated splits). A plain t-interval over 50
  fold scores treated as independent is too narrow; a corrected resampled
  t (Nadeau & Bengio 2003; Bouckaert & Frank 2004) or an equivalent
  justified choice is acceptable, with its limitation stated. A t-interval
  over the 10 repetition means is NOT acceptable as the primary interval:
  every repetition uses the same 150 points, so it measures only partition
  noise (on this study it gives LR-kNN [+0.47, +1.39] pp, excluding 0, where
  the corrected interval is [-2.51, +4.38]).
- The text's conclusion follows from the intervals (no claim of a
  difference the interval does not support, either way). No conclusion is
  required in advance.

## R5 Ibex and local runs
- The full CV (all 50 folds, both methods, inner k selection) run once as a
  CPU job on Ibex and once locally, from the same code.
- Ibex node name reported, backed by the job's own output (SLURM job id,
  `hostname`/`SLURM_JOB_NODELIST` captured by the job) kept in the delivery
  or the analysis repository.
- Whether the two runs give identical numbers is stated and backed by a
  comparison of the per-fold results (not only the summary), with library
  versions of both environments recorded; any difference explained.

## R6 Report
- `report/report.pdf`, compiled from the delivered LaTeX with the official
  NeurIPS 2026 style (submission/anonymous mode), at most two pages as
  delivered.
- Exactly one figure and one table, each referenced and discussed in the
  text; LaTeX source and the code that produced every number delivered
  next to the PDF.
- Re-compiling the delivered source reproduces the PDF (no missing files,
  no unresolved references/citations, no overfull boxes running off the page).

## R7 Figure and table
- Figure opened and inspected: legible at print size, axes labelled with
  units, legend or caption identifies every mark, shows what the text says
  (e.g. per-fold/per-repetition accuracies or paired differences with the
  intervals); not blank, placeholder or truncated.
- Table: the reported means, intervals, paired difference, and the
  Ibex/local comparison, with the interval method named.

## R8 Traceability
- Every number in the text, table and figure matches a result file produced
  by the delivered code; re-running the delivered code locally reproduces
  them.
- Citations real and correctly attributed.
